[38;2;124;124;124mNow let me think about the mathematics.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a question about minimal surfaces in hyperbolic 3-manifolds. The key [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconcepts:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- M is convex-cocompact hyperbolic 3-manifold[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- f:S→M is a minimal immersion[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- f(S) is negatively curved (with the induced metric)[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- All closed geodesics in f(S) (as curves on the surface with its intrinsic [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmetric) are closed geodesics in M[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Question: Is f(S) totally geodesic?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about what "closed geodesics in f(S)" means. The surface f(S) has [0m
[38;2;124;124;124man induced metric from M. A closed geodesic in f(S) is a closed curve that is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgeodesic with respect to the intrinsic metric on f(S). The condition says these [0m[38;2;124;124;124mare also geodesics in the ambient M.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor a surface in a 3-manifold, a curve on the surface that is geodesic in the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msurface is geodesic in the ambient manifold if and only if the geodesic [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcurvature vanishes, which by the Gauss formula relates to the second fundamental[0m[38;2;124;124;124mform.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me recall: if γ is a curve on a surface Σ in a 3-manifold, and γ is geodesic[0m[38;2;124;124;124min Σ, then γ is geodesic in M iff the normal curvature of γ vanishes, i.e., [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mII(γ', γ') = 0 where II is the second fundamental form.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the condition "all closed geodesics in f(S) are geodesics in M" means: for [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mevery closed geodesic γ in f(S), II(γ', γ') = 0.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, the question is whether this forces II = 0 (totally geodesic).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKey theorem: This is related to results by various authors. Let me think about [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwhat's known.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor a negatively curved surface, the geodesic flow is Anosov, and closed [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgeodesics are dense in the unit tangent bundle (in a suitable sense). Actually, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mfor a negatively curved closed surface, the closed geodesics are dense in the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgeodesic flow.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe second fundamental form II is a symmetric bilinear form on the tangent [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mspace. At each point p, we can diagonalize it: II = λ₁ e₁⊗e₁ + λ₂ e₂⊗e₂ where [0m
[38;2;124;124;124me₁, e₂ are principal directions.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA geodesic γ in the surface with unit tangent making angle θ with e₁ has II(γ', [0m[38;2;124;124;124mγ') = λ₁ cos²θ + λ₂ sin²θ.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor this to vanish for all closed geodesics passing through p... but we need to [0m[38;2;124;124;124mbe careful. The condition is that for each closed geodesic, II vanishes along [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe entire geodesic.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, the key insight: on a negatively curved closed surface, the closed [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgeodesics are dense in the unit tangent bundle. This is a classical result [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(Anosov closing lemma). [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo if II(γ', γ') = 0 for all closed geodesics, and closed geodesics are dense in[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe unit tangent bundle, then by continuity, II(v, v) = 0 for all unit vectors v[0m[38;2;124;124;124min the unit tangent bundle. This means II = 0, i.e., the surface is totally [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgeodesic.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, let me be more precise. The function (p, v) ↦ II_p(v, v) is a continuous [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfunction on the unit tangent bundle of f(S). The closed geodesics are dense in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe unit tangent bundle (for negatively curved surfaces, by the Anosov closing [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mlemma / specification property). If this function vanishes on all closed [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgeodesics, and closed geodesics are dense, then the function vanishes [0m
[38;2;124;124;124meverywhere.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me be more careful. The set of unit tangent vectors that lie on [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mclosed geodesics is dense in the unit tangent bundle T¹(f(S)). This is a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconsequence of the Anosov closing lemma for geodesic flows on negatively curved [0m[38;2;124;124;124msurfaces.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe function F: T¹(f(S)) → ℝ defined by F(p, v) = II_p(v, v) is continuous. For [0m[38;2;124;124;124many closed geodesic γ, F vanishes along γ (by the hypothesis). Since the union [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mof closed geodesics is dense in T¹(f(S)), F vanishes on a dense set, hence F = 0[0m[38;2;124;124;124meverywhere.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mF(p, v) = 0 for all unit v means II_p(v,v) = 0 for all unit v, which means II_p [0m[38;2;124;124;124m= 0 for all p. So f(S) is totally geodesic.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the answer is YES.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me make sure about the density of closed geodesics. For a closed Riemannian [0m[38;2;124;124;124msurface with negative curvature, the geodesic flow is Anosov (this is a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mclassical result). The Anosov closing lemma implies that periodic orbits are [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdense in the non-wandering set, which for an Anosov flow is the entire manifold [0m[38;2;124;124;124m(here T¹Σ). So closed geodesics are dense in T¹Σ.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, more precisely: the set of vectors tangent to closed geodesics is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdense in T¹Σ. This is a standard consequence of the Anosov closing lemma.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the proof is:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. f(S) is a closed negatively curved surface, so its geodesic flow is Anosov.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. By the Anosov closing lemma, closed geodesics are dense in the unit tangent [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mbundle T¹(f(S)).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m3. The second fundamental form II defines a continuous function F on T¹(f(S)) by[0m[38;2;124;124;124mF(p,v) = II_p(v,v).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m4. A curve γ on f(S) that is geodesic in f(S) is geodesic in M iff II(γ',γ') = 0[0m[38;2;124;124;124m(by the Gauss formula / geodesic curvature decomposition).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m5. By hypothesis, all closed geodesics in f(S) are geodesics in M, so F = 0 on [0m
